WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.700
The new Galaxy S10 is here and it’s packing
some pretty impressive battery life, but what

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.240
if there was a way to significantly increase
the amount of battery life you can get out

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.240
of it?

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.010
Well, from the results I found in this test,
there is, and it’s by using Android 9 Pie’s

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.140
new Night Mode, which you can easily toggle
from the notification panel.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.960
Many of you know that the S10 uses an OLED
display, just like many other phones on the

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.539
market, but what you might not know is that
displaying black or dark pixels uses much

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:28.050
less battery power compared to white ones.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.610
This test by senk9 shows just how much more
power the color white takes on OLED displays

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:38.600
compared to black, and with red and green
taking significantly less power than blue.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.860
Now if you’ve got a phone with an LCD display,
like the iPhone XR, this won’t work because

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.309
unlike OLED, it lights up the whole display
panel.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:52.510
With OLED, each pixel is individually backlit,
so if it’s displaying true black, it’s

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.280
completely turned off and isn’t using any
power.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.019
So to test this out, we displayed the Reddit
homepage, which is almost completely white,

00:01:00.019 --> 00:01:05.040
and measured the battery drain after 2 hours,
and then we did the same with Samsung’s

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.180
Night Mode enabled.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:11.820
Before we began, we closed background apps,
set the power mode to optimized, turned off

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.591
the blue light filter, turned off adaptive
brightness, and set the brightness to the

00:01:16.591 --> 00:01:20.790
max to show how big of a difference Night
Mode can make if you love maxing out your

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:21.790
brightness.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.240
I used Samsung’s browser for this since
Chrome isn’t currently affected by Night

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:30.250
Mode, but Android Q is bringing a dark mode
to Chrome soon, so that’s pretty exciting.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.370
Now let’s get into our battery test.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:36.350
After spending 2 hours on the Reddit homepage
with Night Mode turned on, the battery went

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:42.280
from 100% to 92%, which is actually really
impressive considering the phone was at maximum

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.280
brightness.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.070
We then charged it back up to 100%, turned
off Night Mode and ran the test again.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:51.850
This time, the battery went from 100% to a
much lower 80%.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.060
That’s a pretty decent difference for simply
turning on Night Mode, and that’s with the

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.670
Power Mode set to Optimized.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:02.380
Now imagine if we used High-Performance mode,
which makes the display even brighter!

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.300
Also, if we would have extended this test
until the battery drained, just enabling Night

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:12.170
Mode would make the battery last an extra
15 hours compared to having it disabled.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:16.230
So anyone who does a lot of browsing on bright
or white pages should definitely take advantage

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.650
of Night Mode to save some extra battery life.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:21.080
But wait, there are more ways to save battery
life.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.920
Simply using a black or dark wallpaper compared
to the stock wallpaper will help save battery

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.000
life as well.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.630
Senk9 also tested brightness versus battery
consumption, and if you look at this graph,

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:35.860
you’ll see that battery drain shot through
the roof when above 80% brightness, so make

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.510
sure you don’t go above that if you care
about battery life.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:42.970
Even better, just use adaptive brightness
and it’ll adjust it to match your environment.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:46.060
This way, you’re not wasting battery power
when you’re indoors.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.640
Another important thing to mention is that
you should set your screen timeout duration

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.850
to as short as is convenient for you.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:55.620
If you set it to 10 minutes and constantly
forget to turn off your screen, your battery

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.050
will drain much quicker.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:01.000
Finally, if you’re running low on battery
and need to stretch it out a few more hours,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.319
Samsung’s built-in battery saving mode works
incredibly well.

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:07.450
If this video helped you out, make sure to
like and subscribe so you don’t miss out

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:08.670
on more videos like this one.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:14.240
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next video!

